<header><p class="enc-kicker">Institution</p><h1 class="enc-title">Der Spiegel</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">1947</span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Der Spiegel is a German weekly news magazine founded in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hanover">Hanover</a> in 1947 and published in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hamburg">Hamburg</a> since 1952, led for decades by Rudolf Augstein. Known for investigative journalism, it was at the centre of the Spiegel affair of 1962, when its offices were searched and editors arrested over a report on West German defences. The resulting outcry strengthened press freedom in the Federal Republic.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/der-spiegel.jpg" alt="Der Spiegel" width="320" height="428" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Spiegel-Verlag · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Der_Spiegel_15Feb_1988.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
